"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","sourcePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Horloge_Gare_Lyon_Paris_26.jpg","sourceSha1":"a980746cff0a033d8c39bbc405514097180ecac9","use":"visual-reference-only","view":"Close daytime exterior confirms stone shaft, projecting four-sided clock stage, convex mansard crown and small lantern. Not the Palais de Justice clock tower."}],"aliases":[],"authors":["Open Landmarks contributors"],"artisticLicense":"CC-BY-4.0","spatialDataLicense":"ODbL-1.0","licenseScope":"CC BY applies to original artistic contributions only; ODbL applies to OSM-derived spatial data. Reference photographs retain their own licenses.","assistance":"AI-assisted architectural interpretation and procedural recipe authoring; deterministic Python/Blender export. No photographic textures.","review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"checks":{}},"modelingNotes":["The clock tower is modeled independently of Gare de Lyon. OSM supplies the 35–50 m clock stage, 63 m main roof and 67 m lantern. The main roof part starts at ground in the source; the outline fills the remaining lower shaft to 35 m.","The inspected close view shows four large circular dials, a heavy cornice, curved mansard crown and small lantern. A shared faceted dome profile approximates the convex main crown within its mapped plan; 3.2 m clock radius, 42.5 m clock centers and cornice thickness are visual inferences.","The 35 m lower-shaft fill is an explicit interpretation of the floating clock-stage minimum height and the observed continuous shaft. Dial marks use existing clock primitives; sculptures and fine roof ornament are omitted."],"source":{"url":"/objects/50c9ca9c2e4ef60ccb29bb187c895fcb775dbe5b006d6cbcc61ba861ac06e0ab/source.blend.gz","bytes":118548,"sha256":"50c9ca9c2e4ef60ccb29bb187c895fcb775dbe5b006d6cbcc61ba861ac06e0ab"},"spatialSource":{"url":"/objects/9efc478c0ab4eafbfc196ec11753270873bab54a4a0e9cd576d80f635df148e4/spatial-source.jso